## Lore

Roadgangers are the mounted raiders of the Outback: clans descended from
the desert towns and trucker stops that survived the Cataclysm intact and
then had to fight off waves of refugees to keep their fuel, food and
weapons. Generations later that defence has hardened into a culture of
raiding. Gangs of a dozen to several hundred roll across the wastelands in
pieced-together cars, bikes and trucks, taking what they want and extorting
tribute from settlements too weak to refuse.

They dress to frighten: wild hair, spiked and studded armor hung with bones
and pre-Rifts junk, war masks, and vehicles painted with flames and skulls.
Most are capable mechanics who love their machines; a minority ride Giant
Kang instead, especially where fuel is scarce.

A player Roadganger has turned away from that life or been cast out of it.
Raised to settle things with force, slow to trust and unused to sharing, the
character has to learn other habits from companions, while the people of the
Outback go on fearing him and his old gang regards him as a traitor.

## GM Notes

The book heads this class an NPC villain and an optional player character,
allowed at the G.M.''s discretion. NPC Roadgangers are overwhelmingly
Diabolic, Miscreant or Anarchist.

Not applied by the sheet: +1 melee attack/action while riding a vehicle, and
no penalty for shooting from a moving vehicle.

Vehicle: a motorcycle, pickup truck or jeep-type vehicle, plus $20,000 to
spend on improving it. Whatever is not spent on the vehicle is lost, not
kept as cash. The vehicle carries very basic tools; a better tool set comes
out of the vehicle budget.

Starting kit beyond what the sheet lists: one or two M.D. weapons and a
couple of S.D.C. weapons matching the character''s W.P.s, a camp stove, and a
kit bag of crude cutlery, a half empty box of matches, bandages and whatever
else the G.M. allows.

Money is 1D4x100 in cash plus 2D6x100 in saleable or tradeable items.
Australian dollars equal credits.
', 'published', 'data-script'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'roadganger');


-- Read the result back rather than trusting the exit code. d1-apply prints
-- these, and a CR in the stored markdown means the checkout mangled the file.
SELECT class_id, name, status, length(markdown) AS bytes, instr(markdown, char(13)) > 0 AS has_cr
  FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'roadganger';

